<h2>Phase 1: Planning (Points 1–10) — The Foundation That Determines Everything</h2>

<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> The planning phase of a website redesign involves auditing your current site, defining measurable goals, analyzing competitors, mapping your customer journey, documenting existing assets, setting a realistic budget and timeline, assembling your team, choosing your platform, planning your content, and establishing your measurement framework. Skip any of these, and your redesign is built on sand.</p>

<p>After managing 200+ contractor redesigns, we can say with absolute certainty: <strong>planning determines 70% of your redesign success.</strong> The contractors who spend two weeks in planning launch faster, rank higher, and convert better than those who rush to design. Here is your 10-point planning checklist.</p>

<h3>Point 1: Audit Your Current Website Performance</h3>

<p>Before you change a single pixel, you need to know what is working and what is not. We start every redesign with a comprehensive audit covering:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Traffic sources:</strong> Where do your visitors come from? Organic search, Google Ads, direct, referral?</li>
<li><strong>Top-performing pages:</strong> Which pages drive the most calls and form submissions?</li>
<li><strong>Conversion rates:</strong> What percentage of visitors contact you? Industry average for home services is 2–5%.</li>
<li><strong>Page speed:</strong> Use Google PageSpeed Insights. Anything below 70 on mobile needs fixing.</li>
<li><strong>Current keyword rankings:</strong> Document every page 1 ranking you have. You will protect these during the redesign.</li>
</ul>

<p>One roofing contractor we worked with in Dallas discovered that 40% of their leads came from a single blog post about "hail damage roof repair." Without the audit, they might have deleted that post during the redesign. Instead, we made it a cornerstone page and increased its traffic 180%.</p>

<h3>Point 2: Define Specific, Measurable Goals</h3>

<p>"We want a better website" is not a goal. "Increase form submissions 40% in 90 days" is. Every goal in your website redesign checklist should be SMART:</p>

