      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h3>Point 3: Analyze Your Top 3 Competitors</h3>

<p>Do not copy your competitors. Study them to find gaps you can exploit. For each competitor, document:</p>

<ol>
<li>Their top 5 ranking pages (use Ahrefs or SEMrush)</li>
<li>Their call-to-action strategy — where do they place "Get a Quote" buttons?</li>
<li>Their service area coverage — which cities do they target?</li>
<li>Their review count and average rating on Google</li>
<li>Their page load speed (use GTmetrix)</li>
</ol>

<p>A plumbing client in Phoenix discovered their biggest competitor had no dedicated page for "tankless water heater installation." We built one, optimized it, and that page alone generated $47,000 in new business in its first year.</p>

<h3>Point 4: Map Your Customer Journey</h3>

<p>Home service customers move through distinct stages. Your website redesign steps must address each one:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Awareness:</strong> "My AC is making a weird noise" — they need educational content</li>
<li><strong>Consideration:</strong> "Should I repair or replace my AC?" — they need comparison guides</li>
<li><strong>Decision:</strong> "Who should I hire?" — they need trust signals and easy contact options</li>
<li><strong>Post-purchase:</strong> "How do I maintain this?" — they need follow-up content that drives reviews and referrals</li>
</ul>

<h3>Point 5: Inventory Your Existing Content and Assets</h3>

<p>Create a spreadsheet of every page, image, video, and PDF on your current site. Mark each as:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Keep:</strong> High-performing content that stays as-is</li>
<li><strong>Update:</strong> Content with potential that needs refreshing</li>
<li><strong>Consolidate:</strong> Thin pages that should merge into stronger ones</li>
<li><strong>Remove:</strong> Outdated or irrelevant content</li>
</ul>

<h3>Point 6: Set a Realistic Budget and Timeline</h3>

